What is a Stream?
A Stream is a pipeline used to process collections (List, Set, Arrays) in a clean, readable, functional style.
Streams are non-mutable

Stream Components:
A stream has 3 main parts:
Source – The data
Intermediate Operations – Transform/filter the data
Terminal Operations – Produce the final result

1. Stream Source:
Stream is created from collections.
Syntax:
list.stream()
set.stream()
Arrays.stream(array)
Stream.of("A", "B")

Example:
List<Integer> nums = List.of(1, 2, 3, 4);
Stream<Integer> s = nums.stream();

2. Intermediate Operations:
These return a new stream.
They don’t give final output directly.
1) filter() → Keep only matching elements
2) map() → Convert values
3) sorted() → Sort
4) distinct() → Remove duplicates
5) limit() / skip() → Reduce stream size

3. Terminal Operations
These end the stream.
Only one terminal operations allowed.

forEach() – print/process each element
collect() – convert back to List/Set
count() – number of elements
findFirst(), findAny()
anyMatch(), allMatch(), noneMatch()
reduce() – combine elements into one value

| Component          | Purpose                | Returns    |
| ------------------ | ---------------------- | ---------- |
| **Source**         | Start of stream        | Stream     |
| **filter()**       | Keep some values       | Stream     |
| **map()**          | Transform values       | Stream     |
| **sorted()**       | Sort values            | Stream     |
| **distinct()**     | Remove duplicates      | Stream     |
| **limit()/skip()** | Slice stream           | Stream     |
| **forEach()**      | Final processing       | void       |
| **collect()**      | Convert to list, set   | Collection |
| **count()**        | Count items            | long       |
| **reduce()**       | Reduce to single value | Optional   |

Using IntStream:
//Doubling Each number
IntStream.of(1, 2, 3)
         .map(n -> n * 2)
         .forEach(System.out::println);
//Int to Double
IntStream.of(1, 2, 3)
         .mapToDouble(n -> n * 2.5)
         .forEach(System.out::println);

CHARACTER OPERATIONS IN STREAMS:
Java Streams do not directly support Stream<Character> from a string,
but we can create it easily.

Creating Character Stream:
String name = "Murali";
name.chars()
    .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
    .forEach(System.out::println);

Filter only vowels:
String name = "Murali";
name.chars()
    .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
    .filter(ch -> "aeiouAEIOU".indexOf(ch) != -1)
    .forEach(System.out::println); //u a i

To UpperCase:
name.chars()
    .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
    .map(Character::toUpperCase)
    .forEach(System.out::println);

Sort Characters:
name.chars()
    .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
    .sorted()
    .forEach(System.out::println);

Remove space from sentence:
String sentence = "M u r a l i";

sentence.chars()
    .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
    .filter(ch -> ch != ' ')
    .forEach(System.out::println);

Count Vowels:
long count =
    name.chars()
        .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
        .filter(ch -> "aeiouAEIOU".contains(ch + ""))
        .count();

System.out.println(count);






